1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,033
This is an overview of SEAVISTA,

2
00:00:03,033 --> 00:00:06,733
a research project built
at FORTH-ICS and co-funded

3
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:10,633
by Frontex under the Frontex
Research Grants Programme.

4
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:15,367
It asks what it would take for one officer
to manage a whole sea with confidence.

5
00:00:15,367 --> 00:00:17,732
Deep dives to come.

6
00:00:17,732 --> 00:00:20,367
This is real shipping, recorded.

7
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:24,267
2 days of Danish waters, as the
vessels themselves reported it.

8
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:28,057
Every track is a vessel saying
where it is, every few seconds.

9
00:00:28,057 --> 00:00:32,124
Watching it takes a team today, and
it is still more than a team can hold.

10
00:00:32,124 --> 00:00:34,100
Almost all of it is ordinary.

11
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:36,033
A ferry keeping its timetable.

12
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:38,415
A tanker on the line it always takes.

13
00:00:38,415 --> 00:00:43,156
The job is to find the one that
stands out, while it still matters.

14
00:00:43,156 --> 00:00:45,200
Today that is done with rules.

15
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,874
Draw a zone, set a speed limit,
wait for something to cross it.

16
00:00:48,874 --> 00:00:50,700
Leave it loose and it misses.

17
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,516
Draw it tight and it fires on the
ferry, every day, on schedule.

18
00:00:54,516 --> 00:00:58,335
And every new trick needs a new
rule, written after it has worked.

19
00:00:58,335 --> 00:01:01,868
The sea changes faster
than the rulebook does.

20
00:01:01,868 --> 00:01:06,955
SEAVISTA never waits to be asked. Every
vessel is scored on every report it sends.

21
00:01:06,955 --> 00:01:09,000
3 of the questions it asks.

22
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,933
Is this manoeuvre ordinary?

23
00:01:10,933 --> 00:01:14,433
Is it an ordinary continuation
of what this vessel was doing?

24
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:16,073
Do vessels ever come here?

25
00:01:16,073 --> 00:01:20,400
These are 3 parts of one calculation,
and it has room for more.

26
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,252
New evidence goes in as another
term, and the model carries on.

27
00:01:24,252 --> 00:01:26,833
A position tells you
where a vessel has been.

28
00:01:26,833 --> 00:01:29,113
Every decision is about where it will be.

29
00:01:29,113 --> 00:01:30,900
One model gives both.

30
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:35,400
A curve fitted to the track, a learned
density over where such curves go.

31
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,533
Run it forward and you reason
over possible outcomes,

32
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:41,667
each one weighed by how often
vessels have gone there.

33
00:01:41,667 --> 00:01:43,927
A learned probability, not a rule.

34
00:01:43,927 --> 00:01:46,797
Where it can go depends on
more than its own heading.

35
00:01:46,797 --> 00:01:48,200
The traffic around it.

36
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,500
The water under it.

37
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:51,167
The coast, the ports.

38
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:54,067
And an intent, the
destination it broadcasts.

39
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:57,707
All of it inputs to one
calculation, not rules in a list.

40
00:01:57,707 --> 00:02:02,390
So the same turn is routine in a shipping
lane, and worth a look in empty water.

41
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:07,247
And the agent runs it for itself,
in the middle of its own reasoning.

42
00:02:07,247 --> 00:02:11,979
When something stands out, an agent works
the case before any officer is called.

43
00:02:11,979 --> 00:02:15,243
The agent writes its own
investigation, and runs it.

44
00:02:15,243 --> 00:02:17,133
It can question the live picture,

45
00:02:17,133 --> 00:02:18,400
run the forecast,

46
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:19,933
plot this vessel's scores,

47
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:21,767
and draw the water on a map.

48
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:23,400
Then it looks at what it drew,

49
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:26,866
and reasons about the picture the
way it reasons about the numbers.

50
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:29,133
It can ask who operates this vessel,

51
00:02:29,133 --> 00:02:30,800
what was reported nearby,

52
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:32,716
what this watch has already cleared.

53
00:02:32,716 --> 00:02:35,567
The next case gets a
different investigation.

54
00:02:35,567 --> 00:02:39,841
Different questions, in a different
order, chosen after seeing the evidence.

55
00:02:39,841 --> 00:02:42,527
A rulebook cannot do that.

56
00:02:42,527 --> 00:02:46,367
Without a layer like this, every
alert is somebody's problem.

57
00:02:46,367 --> 00:02:49,200
The queue grows faster
than a watch can clear it,

58
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:51,452
and the cost of looking falls on people.

59
00:02:51,452 --> 00:02:54,233
With it, one officer works a short list.

60
00:02:54,233 --> 00:02:57,067
Every case on it has
already been investigated,

61
00:02:57,067 --> 00:03:00,399
and arrives with its reasons attached.

62
00:03:00,399 --> 00:03:04,333
Across 3 passes over 2 labelled
sets of ordinary traffic,

63
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:06,833
it raised no false alarm at all.

64
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:09,591
A system that cries
wolf gets switched off.

65
00:03:09,591 --> 00:03:11,500
That quiet is engineered.

66
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:14,100
The scoring layer is deliberately eager.

67
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:16,467
It flags anything that stands out.

68
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:19,800
Every flag then goes to an
agent with the full context,

69
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:24,058
so a real case reaches the
operator already explained.

70
00:03:24,058 --> 00:03:29,033
SEAVISTA is a research prototype, and
all of it runs on one workstation.

71
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:34,133
About 6,000 vessel-cycles a second,
every one a full pass through the models.

72
00:03:34,133 --> 00:03:35,700
Spend it as you like.

73
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:40,229
1,200 vessels at 5 refreshes
a second, or 3,000 at 2.

74
00:03:40,229 --> 00:03:42,233
Aggregate the requests and the same

75
00:03:42,233 --> 00:03:45,867
hardware sustains about
48 times that throughput.

76
00:03:45,867 --> 00:03:48,367
It was tested end to end on one sea,

77
00:03:48,367 --> 00:03:52,615
and assessed for generalisation
across 4 national-scale corpora.

78
00:03:52,615 --> 00:03:54,933
Under load, with queueing counted in,

79
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:58,800
an alert is in front of a person
in a median of 84 seconds,

80
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,867
investigated and explained,

81
00:04:00,867 --> 00:04:02,290
ready for their decision.

82
00:04:02,290 --> 00:04:04,733
Nothing becomes final without a person.

83
00:04:04,733 --> 00:04:06,467
The system narrows the sea.

84
00:04:06,467 --> 00:04:08,880
The officer decides.

85
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:12,533
SEAVISTA has run end to
end on recorded traffic.

86
00:04:12,533 --> 00:04:16,533
The parts that would let one officer
manage a whole sea are now built,

87
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:17,667
and measured.

88
00:04:17,667 --> 00:04:19,867
It was built at FORTH-ICS,

89
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:25,433
in its Computational Vision
and Robotics Laboratory.
